1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,241
This is the longer look at Soundings.

2
00:00:02,291 --> 00:00:04,956
What it's for, how it works, the statistics behind it,

3
00:00:04,976 --> 00:00:08,309
and why you can trust what it shows, and where you shouldn't.

4
00:00:08,906 --> 00:00:13,153
The usual way is to pick a signal, then check what price did after it.

5
00:00:13,203 --> 00:00:16,191
But you only ever test the ideas you already had.

6
00:00:16,241 --> 00:00:17,614
Soundings turns it round.

7
00:00:17,634 --> 00:00:21,725
It marks every past move first, then looks back at what came before.

8
00:00:21,775 --> 00:00:24,037
The data suggests the ideas.

9
00:00:24,483 --> 00:00:26,511
First, what counts as a move.

10
00:00:26,561 --> 00:00:28,723
A rise means price reaches two typical

11
00:00:28,743 --> 00:00:31,422
moves above the close within six daily candles,

12
00:00:31,442 --> 00:00:34,498
without first falling one typical move below it.

13
00:00:34,548 --> 00:00:39,200
A fall is the mirror image. A candle that touches both counts as neither.

14
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:44,835
A typical move is that coin's own average daily range, over the last twenty candles.

15
00:00:44,885 --> 00:00:48,705
So a rise means the same thing for a calm coin and a wild one.

16
00:00:49,147 --> 00:00:53,031
Then, at the close before every day, it reads the app's own signals.

17
00:00:53,081 --> 00:00:57,562
Trend, stretch, flow, chart patterns, Sonar, market structure, and the Tides.

18
00:00:57,612 --> 00:01:01,073
That's a hundred and twenty seven conditions, and pairs of them:

19
00:01:01,093 --> 00:01:02,862
over seven thousand in all.

20
00:01:02,912 --> 00:01:05,640
Each is replayed exactly as it stood at that close,

21
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,332
so nothing can see the move that came after.

22
00:01:08,803 --> 00:01:11,706
Each condition is compared with the usual rate.

23
00:01:11,756 --> 00:01:14,756
If rises usually follow twenty one percent of closes,

24
00:01:14,776 --> 00:01:17,207
and thirty one percent after the condition,

25
00:01:17,227 --> 00:01:20,034
that's a lift of about one and a half times.

26
00:01:20,084 --> 00:01:21,870
And usual means comparable days:

27
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:26,422
the same coin, the same year, the same side of its two hundred day average.

28
00:01:26,472 --> 00:01:30,932
So a bull year, or a coin that only went up, can't make a condition look good.

29
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:33,493
The history is split in three.

30
00:01:33,543 --> 00:01:37,726
The search years, to the end of twenty twenty two, where ideas are found.

31
00:01:37,776 --> 00:01:42,961
The later years, to October twenty twenty four: a fresh exam the search never saw.

32
00:01:43,011 --> 00:01:45,904
And a sealed hold-out from then on, never read at all.

33
00:01:45,924 --> 00:01:49,029
It's kept for the final test of a finished strategy.

34
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:50,953
Here's the trap.

35
00:01:51,003 --> 00:01:54,855
Test three hundred useless ideas at the usual one in twenty standard,

36
00:01:54,875 --> 00:01:56,914
and about fifteen pass by luck.

37
00:01:56,964 --> 00:01:59,013
Soundings tries thousands.

38
00:01:59,063 --> 00:02:01,607
So it uses the Benjamini Hochberg correction:

39
00:02:01,627 --> 00:02:04,718
at most ten percent of what it keeps may be luck.

40
00:02:04,768 --> 00:02:08,462
And a result needs at least one and a quarter times the usual rate,

41
00:02:08,482 --> 00:02:11,276
on a hundred and fifty days, and eight coins.

42
00:02:11,738 --> 00:02:13,425
There's a second trap.

43
00:02:13,475 --> 00:02:16,527
Coins move together, and neighbouring days overlap.

44
00:02:16,577 --> 00:02:21,805
A hundred coins rising on the same day isn't a hundred separate pieces of evidence.

45
00:02:21,855 --> 00:02:26,409
So the test reshuffles whole blocks of calendar days, across all coins at once,

46
00:02:26,429 --> 00:02:27,829
thousands of times.

47
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:31,017
The crowding stays in, and nothing is counted twice.

48
00:02:31,463 --> 00:02:35,283
For crypto rises, a thousand and four results passed the search.

49
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:37,916
But most are one idea in many forms.

50
00:02:37,966 --> 00:02:39,622
So they're grouped into themes,

51
00:02:39,642 --> 00:02:43,727
and only the strongest of each theme sits the exam on the later years.

52
00:02:43,777 --> 00:02:44,952
Once.

53
00:02:45,393 --> 00:02:49,042
Of the twenty theme leaders for crypto rises, three held up.

54
00:02:49,092 --> 00:02:54,299
Ten came before more rises and more falls: a bigger move coming, direction unknown.

55
00:02:54,349 --> 00:02:56,185
Five were weaker later.

56
00:02:56,235 --> 00:02:58,242
And two faded completely.

57
00:02:58,694 --> 00:03:03,474
In the app, those verdicts group the cards on the Before rises tab, strongest first.

58
00:03:04,667 --> 00:03:06,418
Take one that held up.

59
00:03:06,468 --> 00:03:11,632
Price near the top of its Bollinger band, with the two Stoch RSI lines far apart.

60
00:03:11,682 --> 00:03:15,421
In the search years, a rise followed thirty one percent of the time,

61
00:03:15,441 --> 00:03:17,123
against twenty one usual.

62
00:03:17,173 --> 00:03:19,890
On the later years, twenty seven against twenty,

63
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:23,574
and it was higher on seventy one coins out of ninety seven.

64
00:03:23,624 --> 00:03:27,888
Smaller on the exam than in the search: that's normal. A real edge shrinks.

65
00:03:27,908 --> 00:03:29,407
A lucky one vanishes.

66
00:03:29,857 --> 00:03:31,864
So, the integrity checks.

67
00:03:31,914 --> 00:03:35,053
The coins include ones that collapsed or stopped trading,

68
00:03:35,073 --> 00:03:37,312
so it isn't only today's survivors.

69
00:03:37,362 --> 00:03:41,929
Every reading is as it stood at the time, and macro data as first published.

70
00:03:41,979 --> 00:03:44,711
Cut points are fixed in advance, never tuned.

71
00:03:44,761 --> 00:03:48,316
It's corrected for every try, it must hold across many coins,

72
00:03:48,336 --> 00:03:50,160
the hold-out stays sealed,

73
00:03:50,210 --> 00:03:52,302
and the failures stay on the page.

74
00:03:52,745 --> 00:03:54,197
Now the limits.

75
00:03:54,247 --> 00:03:57,107
Even that result means no rise most of the time.

76
00:03:57,127 --> 00:04:02,227
A rise followed twenty seven times in a hundred. Seventy three times, it didn't.

77
00:04:02,277 --> 00:04:06,652
Costs and slippage aren't included. It's daily candles only, so far.

78
00:04:06,702 --> 00:04:10,138
And markets change. Soundings describes the past.

79
00:04:10,604 --> 00:04:12,867
You can check any result by eye.

80
00:04:12,917 --> 00:04:16,128
The chart puts each row under the price, with the Tide on top,

81
00:04:16,148 --> 00:04:17,803
for any coin and any year.

82
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:26,319
So use a result as a starting point.

83
00:04:26,369 --> 00:04:30,999
Open it as a Signal Set. Scan for it with Prism. Narrow it in a funnel.

84
00:04:31,049 --> 00:04:35,339
Then bring your own judgement: the chart, your risk, your size, and your exit.
